<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Comparative Analysis: Differences and Significance</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Differences from ES in Supervised Learning</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Salimans et al. (2017) studied, on small supervised datasets (e.g., MNIST), the correlation between ES gradient estimates <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}^{ES}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}^{ES}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained via random perturbations and the true gradients <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}^{True}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}^{True}</annotation></semantics></math> from backpropagation, using the mini-batch performance/loss as a black-box objective <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\theta+\sigma\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\theta+\sigma\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Although that objective is computed from logits, it does not explicitly structure token-level log-probabilities as a reusable ES fitness, nor does it provide a systematic, full-parameter ES optimization for large-scale LLMs in pretraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach explicitly rewrites the language modeling maximum-likelihood objective as ES fitness. Let the model outputs (logits) be <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define</p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x;\theta)=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}m_{t}\cdot\log\mathrm{softmax}\big(z_{t}(\theta)\big)_{y_{t}},\quad F(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}R(x;\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>softmax</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x;\theta)=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}m_{t}\cdot\log\mathrm{softmax}\big(z_{t}(\theta)\big)_{y_{t}},\quad F(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}R(x;\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used for masking or weighting, and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> aligns with the standard log-likelihood objective in language modeling. We use <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> as the ES fitness during pretraining and combine antithetic sampling, rank shaping, grouped/layered noise and step-size controls, and cross-process synchronized subsampled evaluation, making zeroth-order optimization feasible and efficient at LLM scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Differences from ES in the RL Stage</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This line of work centers on GRPO-style rewards and is tailored to RL fine-tuning, where the objective is a task-specific reward function. Its performance heavily depends on reward design and the sampling environment, and it does not directly target the pretraining objective of language modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We apply ES to full-parameter optimization <em id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">starting from pretraining</em>, with fitness defined as the aggregation of token-level log-probabilities consistent with maximum likelihood, independent of external environments or rewards. Grouped and scheduled controls further enable stable updates at large model scales.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Differences from MeZO and Other Zeroth-Order Optimizers</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MeZO is a memory-efficient zeroth-order optimizer that typically uses two function evaluations <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\theta\pm\delta u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\theta\pm\delta u)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate directional derivatives and then updates parameters in a first-order style, with the objective treated as a black-box mini-batch loss. In contrast, we:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">employ <em id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">population-based</em> ES with antithetic sampling, aggregating multiple perturbation directions per iteration;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">explicitly aggregate <em id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">token-level log-softmax</em> as ES fitness, and use z-score/rank shaping to reduce scale sensitivity and outlier effects;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">introduce <em id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouped/layered</em> noise and step-size multipliers (with scheduling of <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>) for controllability and stability;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">use <em id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-process synchronized subsampled evaluation</em> to reduce cost while preserving unbiasedness <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}_{t}}[F_{M}]=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}_{t}}[F_{M}]=F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, our method does not merely treat the mini-batch loss as a black-box; it integrates the <em id="A1.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">structured probabilistic objective</em> of language modeling directly into ES fitness, together with stabilization and engineering strategies for large-scale pretraining.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Model Configuration Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate a Qwen3-style causal language model using the Transformers implementation (<span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3Config</span>) with the following settings, chosen to approximate a <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>1B-parameter configuration while tying input and output embeddings:</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vocabulary size</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="151{,}936" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>151,936</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">151{,}936</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hidden size</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}536" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,536</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}536</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intermediate size</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4{,}096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4,096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4{,}096</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of layers</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math> transformer blocks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attention heads</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="A1.I2.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KV heads</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> (GQA).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Head dimension</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Max position embeddings</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32{,}768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32,768</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32{,}768</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attention bias</span>: <span id="A1.I2.i8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">false</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tie word embeddings</span>: <span id="A1.I2.i9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">true</span> (output head tied to input embeddings).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Use cache</span>: <span id="A1.I2.i10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">false</span> (disabled during ES evaluation).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i11" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attention dropout</span>: <math id="A1.I2.i11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This configuration aligns with the training scripts used in our experiments and balances model capacity, context length, and ES evaluation stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">langley00</span></cite></p>
</div>
